---
import InfoLayout from '../layouts/InfoLayout.astro';
import InfoSection from '../components/InfoSection.astro';
import SpecTable from '../components/SpecTable.astro';
---

<InfoLayout
  path="/how-to-order/"
  title="How to Order | Custom Packaging Process"
  description="What to send with an enquiry, what happens at each of the five stages, and the two approvals required before anything goes to print."
  h1="How to Order"
  eyebrow="Ordering"
  lead="Every order follows the same sequence, whether it is 500 cartons or a twelve-product range. Nothing goes to print until you have approved a die line and a proof in writing."
  ogTitle="Ordering Custom Lip Packaging, Step by Step"
  ogDescription="What we need from you, what you approve, and where each stage can be sped up or slowed down."
  points={['Five stages', 'Two written approvals', 'No catalogue ordering']}
  ctaHeading="Ready to start?"
  ctaBody="Send the container dimensions or a sample, your quantity, and your launch date if one is fixed. That is enough for a written quote."
>
  <InfoSection
    id="what-to-send"
    heading="What to send with your first enquiry"
    intro="You do not need a full specification. These five things are enough for us to quote accurately, and the more of them you have, the fewer questions come back."
  >
    <ol class="ordered">
      <li>
        <strong>The container.</strong> Its dimensions, or better, a physical
        sample posted to us. For a tube, the outside diameter and the height with
        the cap on. For a lipstick, the width at the widest point with the cap
        fitted.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The quantity.</strong> The number you actually want, plus the
        number per design if the job has shade or scent variants.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>How it is sold.</strong> Shelf, peg fixture, e-commerce, gift set
        or wholesale. This changes the structure more than anything else.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Artwork status.</strong> Print-ready, in progress, or nothing yet.
        All three are workable; we just plan differently.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Any fixed date.</strong> If a launch or a planogram reset is
        driving the schedule, say so at enquiry stage rather than at order stage.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">
      Send it through the <a href="/request-a-quote/">full quote request form</a>,
      which asks for these in order, or use the short form on any product page if
      you would rather just start a conversation.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="stages"
    heading="The five stages"
    intro="What happens, who does it, and what you are asked to decide."
  >
    <SpecTable
      caption="Order stages, responsibilities and approvals."
      columns={['Stage', 'We do', 'You do']}
      rows={[
        [
          '1. Quote',
          'Propose board, print process, finishing and structure, and price it at your quantity and the next break',
          'Confirm which option to develop',
        ],
        [
          '2. Die line',
          'Draw the structural die line around your container and issue it as a vector file',
          'Approve the die line in writing, or send it to your designer to build on',
        ],
        [
          '3. Sample',
          'Produce a plain cut-and-creased sample, or an assembled white sample for a rigid box',
          'Test it with your actual product and confirm the fit',
        ],
        [
          '4. Proof',
          'Issue a colour proof on the specified stock, with foil and spot UV positions shown',
          'Approve each design or shade in writing',
        ],
        [
          '5. Production',
          'Print, finish, die-cut, glue, inspect, pack to your case count and dispatch with tracking',
          'Confirm the delivery address and any receiving requirements',
        ],
      ]}
    />
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="approvals"
    heading="The two approvals that matter"
    intro="These are the points where a mistake becomes expensive, so both are handled in writing."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      <strong>The die line approval</strong> fixes the structure. Once a die is
      cut, changing a dimension means a new die. This is why a structural sample
      tested with your real product is worth the few days it adds.
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      <strong>The proof approval</strong> fixes the colour and the content. The
      production run is measured against the approved proof, so a compliance change
      or a colour change afterwards means new plates and a new run. Have your
      regulated copy reviewed before this point, as set out in the
      <a href="/artwork-guidelines/">artwork guidelines</a>.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="reorders"
    heading="Reordering"
    intro="Repeat orders skip most of the work, and that is reflected in the price."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      Once a die line is cut and a proof approved, both are held against your
      account. A reorder is quoted on print and material cost at whatever quantity
      you need, with no die charge and no proofing charge. Tell us at the first
      quote if you expect to reorder on a cycle, because it changes how the tooling
      is quoted.
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      For a range, a single shade or SKU can be reordered on its own. The economics
      change, since a single-SKU reorder loses the benefit of sharing a press sheet,
      but there is no new setup to pay for.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="changes"
    heading="Changing an order in progress"
    intro="What is straightforward and what is not, depending on how far the job has gone."
  >
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li><strong>Before die line approval</strong> — dimensions, structure, board and quantity can all change freely.</li>
      <li><strong>After die line, before proof</strong> — artwork and colour can change. A dimensional change means a new die.</li>
      <li><strong>After proof approval, before production</strong> — a change means a new proof cycle and, if plates are made, new plates.</li>
      <li><strong>Once production has started</strong> — the run cannot be altered. A change is a new order.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      Quantity increases are easier to accommodate before production than after,
      because a second short run costs considerably more per unit than adding to
      the first. If you are uncertain, ask for the price at both quantities in the
      original quote.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>
</InfoLayout>
